<section class="forever-level-story" aria-label="My little Blizzard API story">
<p class="forever-level-kicker">A morning in Azeroth and on Worldnode · 10 November 2026</p>
<h3>“So, what level are you?”</h3>
<p class="forever-level-dialogue"><strong>“14.”</strong> — “Wait, weren't you level 14 this morning?” — <strong>“Yes! But now my Debian server can track my undead paladin through Blizzard's API. Even his professions!”</strong></p>
<p>I was only about 20 percent away from level 15. Other people might have knocked out one more quest. Instead, I spent the entire morning exploring API endpoints, setting up OAuth credentials and building a Python collector for my blog.</p>
<p>While everyone else plays WoW and levels their characters, I sit in my Fish shell on Arch Linux asking my Debian 13 server, Worldnode, whether it knows how my undead paladin in Azeroth is doing. It actually answered: Dennis Hilk, Undead, Paladin, Horde, level 14. Apparently I just keep finding new excuses to make Linux servers do things. 😄</p>
</section>

<h2 id="destromath">How Nebukatneza became nebu</h2>
<p>My first World of Warcraft home was Destromath, a German EU PvP realm. Horde, undead warlock, guild <em>Klinge der Macht</em>. At some point Nebukatneza became <strong>nebu</strong>, and the name followed me well beyond WoW. I’ve written separately about <a href="/blog/why-im-called-nebu/">how I ended up being called nebu</a>.</p>
<p>My warlock’s story carried on after level 60. <strong>I played every expansion from The Burning Crusade through Dragonflight, and Nebukatneza stayed my main.</strong> Sometimes I took a break and came back halfway through an expansion. The continents, talent trees and rules kept changing. Whenever I came back, I still had my warlock.</p>

<h2 id="expansions">From Vanilla to Dragonflight, one expansion at a time</h2>
<p>At first, what I noticed was how much bigger the world got. There was Outland on the other side of the Dark Portal, and then there was Northrend. Over time, WoW also changed how I spent an evening. Dungeon Finder took care of finding a group. Garrisons, artifact weapons and Covenants gave their respective expansions their own progression systems. Mythic+ gave dungeon groups a challenge that kept scaling with higher keystones. Some parts of the game got easier to get into while others gave players more to master.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="WoW expansions and European release dates" tabindex="0">
<table>
<caption>WoW and its first nine expansions by European release date. Features include pre-patch and later additions from the relevant era.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Game or expansion</th><th scope="col">European release</th><th scope="col">What changed</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">World of Warcraft (Vanilla)</th><td><time datetime="2005-02-11">11 Feb 2005</time></td><td>Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms up to level 60. Paladins were an Alliance class.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">The Burning Crusade</th><td><time datetime="2007-01-16">16 Jan 2007</time></td><td>Outland, flying mounts and blood elves. The Horde got its first paladins.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Wrath of the Lich King</th><td><time datetime="2008-11-13">13 Nov 2008</time></td><td>Northrend and the death knight. Achievements arrived in the pre-patch, and Dungeon Finder followed in patch 3.3.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Cataclysm</th><td><time datetime="2010-12-07">7 Dec 2010</time></td><td>Deathwing reshaped many original zones and quests. Goblins and worgen became playable.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Mists of Pandaria</th><td><time datetime="2012-09-25">25 Sep 2012</time></td><td>Pandaria, monks and pet battles. Talents moved to tiers with a handful of choices in each.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Warlords of Draenor</th><td><time datetime="2014-11-13">13 Nov 2014</time></td><td>An alternate Draenor. Garrisons brought buildings, followers and missions together in a player base.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Legion</th><td><time datetime="2016-08-30">30 Aug 2016</time></td><td>Artifact weapons, class order halls, world quests and scaling Mythic+ dungeons.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Battle for Azeroth</th><td><time datetime="2018-08-14">14 Aug 2018</time></td><td>Kul Tiras and Zandalar, allied races and the Heart of Azeroth as a central progression system.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Shadowlands</th><td><time datetime="2020-11-24">24 Nov 2020</time></td><td>A pre-patch level squish, then Covenants, Torghast and a return to a level cap of 60.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Dragonflight</th><td><time datetime="2022-11-29">29 Nov 2022</time></td><td>The Dragon Isles, dragonriding, revamped professions and separate class and specialization talent trees.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>That timeline also shows how often the direction changed. Cataclysm rebuilt the old world around a new leveling experience. Mists simplified talent selection. Dragonflight brought back branching talent trees and gave professions more depth through specializations and crafting orders. Every few years, the developers made a fresh decision about where to ask for more of a player’s time and where to give some back.</p>

<h2 id="dragonflight">Why Dragonflight was the last expansion I bought</h2>
<p>“<strong>Candy Crush meets World of Warcraft — too much bling-bling everywhere.</strong>” That’s how I jokingly put it. Retail had started to feel too bright and too loud for me. Effects, achievement banners and rewards called for my attention so often that I ended up noticing them less. When the next progress bar was already filling before I’d enjoyed the last one, even a proper achievement started to feel a bit ordinary.</p>
<p>In the parts of the game I played, I often felt I needed less patience or skill to earn a reward than I remembered. High Mythic+ keys and Mythic raids still demand practice and coordination. Plenty of players enjoy the more accessible starting points, the bold look and the modern systems. I missed a quieter atmosphere, slower leveling and the satisfaction of getting something I’d spent a while working toward.</p>
<p>Dragonflight was where my own retail journey ended. I didn’t buy the retail expansions that followed it.</p>

<h2 id="classic-era">Back to Classic Era and eight level-60 characters</h2>
<p>Classic Era felt right again. A trip across the world could take up an evening, and if I skipped a week, the dungeon set I’d worked for was still good enough for whatever I planned to do next. Over time I built up eight level-60 characters on Noggenfogger, plus a level-one auction-house orc. He’s the one with the loudest axe in the picture. You have to have priorities.</p>
<figure class="article-figure">
<a href="/assets/blog/gaming-journey/characters/wow-onlyzugzug-auction-orc.webp"><img src="/assets/blog/gaming-journey/characters/wow-onlyzugzug-auction-orc.webp" width="2048" height="1147" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="WoW Classic Era character selection on Noggenfogger (PvP). Eight level-60 characters are listed at right, and the selected character is the level-one orc Onlyzugzug."></a>
<figcaption>My Classic Era roster on Noggenfogger. Eight level-60 characters, with the auction-house orc Onlyzugzug at the bottom. The original also appears in the <a href="/blog/my-gaming-journey-1984-2026/#wow">WoW Classic Era chapter of my gaming journey</a>. <span class="credit">© Dennis Hilk · 2026</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The Nebukatneza in this list carries on the name of my old Destromath warlock. I can’t find that very first character anymore. I look at this roster and still know exactly where “nebu” came from.</p>

<h2 id="forever">WoW Forever and a paladin named Dennis Hilk</h2>
<p>World of Warcraft: Forever launched on 5 November 2026 here in Germany, on the same global launch that fell on 4 November in North America. Blizzard is building it as a permanent version alongside retail and Classic. The original continents are once again the setting for levels 1 through 60, with new quests and reworked zones. The two changes that mattered most to my fresh start were first names and surnames, and the chance to play a Forsaken paladin.</p>
<p>In Vanilla, paladins were Alliance-only. Blood elves brought the class to the Horde in <em>The Burning Crusade</em>. I played a Horde paladin back then and kept the warlock as my main. In Forever I wanted to see how the world felt if I started with the paladin this time. I did at least stick with the undead and the Horde.</p>
{{screenshot}}
<p>I play in the EU in German on the <strong>Normal (PvE)</strong> ruleset. The public profile also showed level 14 when I checked it on 10 November. That number stays in the screenshot as the beginning of this story, even as the profile eventually moves on.</p>

<h2 id="linux">WoW on Linux with Battle.net through Lutris</h2>
<p>On Cthulhu I play on Arch Linux with dwm. For World of Warcraft I launch Battle.net through Lutris and Wine, then install and start the relevant WoW version there. My Steam games go through Steam and Proton. I cover both setups in my <a href="/blog/linux-gaming-steam-proton-lutris/">Linux gaming guide to Steam, Proton and Lutris</a>.</p>

<h2 id="observer-tech">How the WoW Forever Observer works</h2>
<p>The independent Armory describes two delays. Characters appear from level 10 after logging out, and the Armory can still serve an older saved snapshot. A future sync can turn higher levels and newly reported achievements into dated milestones. The moment it happened in-game remains unknown. If a character gains several levels between checks, the history can record only the level actually observed.</p>
<p>Blizzard’s official API returns my Forever character’s level, experience, equipment and professions. On 10 November 2026, I successfully ran the Python collector on my Debian 13 server, Worldnode, and published its first real JSON snapshot. It contains only selected display data: no credentials and no permanent gameplay history. The website shows the last successful sync time; the hourly automated run is configured separately. An older snapshot is not live telemetry.</p>
<p>I first entered Azeroth as Nebukatneza and somewhere along the way came out as nebu. This time it was simpler. After about twenty years, I finally walked in as Dennis Hilk.</p>

